<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ritsuryo Japan</h1><p class="st-meta">701 CE – 1185 CE · capital Heian-kyo (Kyoto)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The classical Japanese imperial state of the Nara and Heian periods, governed by Chinese-style law codes and home to the courtly culture of The Tale of Genji.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taiho code</a> of 701 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> a full system of penal and administrative law modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, completing reforms begun under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yamato">Yamato</a>. In 710 the court moved to the planned capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heijo-kyo">Heijo-kyo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>, and the first histories, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a> of 712 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon shoki</a> of 720, presented the emperors as descendants of the sun goddess.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daijo-kan">Council of State</a>, eight ministries and provincial governors administered land allotted to cultivators in return for taxes and labour. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Shomu</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todai-ji">Todai-ji</a> with its Great Buddha, dedicated in 752. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Kammu</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyo</a> in 794. From the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara</a> regents ruled in the emperor's name, and court women wrote masterpieces in the new kana script.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Private estates eroded the tax base and provincial warriors grew powerful. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> of 1180 to 1185 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> to power and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate</a>, though the imperial court survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ritsuryo-japan.jpg" alt="Ritsuryo Japan" width="320" height="260"><figcaption>Image: Artanisen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map-of-Japan-1183-Heian-Genpei-War.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>701 CE – 1185 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Heian-kyo (Kyoto)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shomu, Kammu, Fujiwara no Michinaga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Japanese, Classical Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nara Japan, Heian Japan, Imperial court of Japan, Ritsuryo state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=943&amp;focus=ritsuryo-japan" aria-label="Ritsuryo Japan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8121 912 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ritsuryo Japan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8441 1168L8457 1190M8441 1168L8531 1157M8441 1168L8496 1036"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8457 1190h0M8531 1157h0M8496 1036h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1168h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Heian-kyo. <a href="/atlas?year=943&amp;focus=ritsuryo-japan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
